  <h2 id="ai-risk">What AI is actually changing for fashion designers</h2>

  <p>
    Set aside both extremes here too: "AI cannot touch real design taste, it is a creative field" and "AI
    is coming for every design job." Neither survives contact with what is actually shifting task by task,
    and the honest picture for this specific field is more reassuring than for many other creative and
    knowledge-work roles.
  </p>

  <p>
    Independent research puts the automation probability for fashion designers at roughly 23%, meaningfully
    lower than many desk-based creative and analytical roles. That does not mean the job stays untouched:
    AI already automates grading, spec documentation, and pattern matching, tasks that used to consume real
    junior-designer hours, and generative tools can now propose first-pass concept sketches from a prompt.
    More than a third of fashion executives already use generative AI somewhere in their workflow, and
    the global AI-in-fashion market is projected to grow from roughly $2.47 billion to about $9.45 billion
    by 2030.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What is shrinking</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Manual grading, spec documentation, and repetitive pattern-matching work with no design ownership behind it.</li>
        <li>First-pass concept sketches and trend-scanning research done entirely by hand, with no AI assist.</li>
        <li>Templated moodboard assembly with no real curation judgment attached.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What is growing</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Aesthetic judgment and cultural read that a generative tool cannot fully own; current AI still struggles to reason about what a specific audience will actually wear and buy.</li>
        <li>Fit-on-body reasoning and construction judgment that stays firmly human, and firmly hands-on.</li>
        <li>The verification layer: knowing which AI-generated concept, trend call, or moodboard to keep, override, or push back on.</li>
        <li>Client and vendor trust relationships that make a design actually get sourced, made, and sold on time.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Researchers describe the realistic model here as "hybrid intelligence": AI speeds up the mechanical,
    repetitive parts of the job, but the designer's taste, cultural understanding, and domain judgment
    still decide what actually ships and sells. The practical takeaway for someone still deciding on this
    field: AI is making designers faster at the routine parts of the job, not making design judgment
    unnecessary. The staged, realistic path is to use CAD and generative moodboarding tools now to compress
    the slow parts of concept development, then, as adoption matures over the next few years, move
    deliberately into the curation and verification layer, catching a culturally tone-deaf AI suggestion,
    defending a fit decision, and using the freed-up time for more client-facing or specialist work instead
    of more routine sketching volume.
  </p>

