    Answer that honestly before the "designer" title's glamour answers it for you.
  </p>

  <h2 id="celebrity-myth">The "celebrity designer" myth vs the real career</h2>

  <p>
    A specific fantasy drives a lot of fashion-design applications: the idea of becoming a household-name
    designer with a signature label, red-carpet dressing credits, and a fashion-week runway show.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      That level of recognition is genuinely rare, statistically closer to a lottery outcome than a
      realistic career target, even among highly talented, hardworking designers. It should not be the
      reason anyone chooses this field. The real, achievable version of a strong fashion design career
      looks much less glamorous and far more repeatable: steady growth inside a brand or export house from
      design assistant to senior designer over several years, developing one clear specialisation, whether
      bridalwear, sustainable fabrics, technical sportswear, or a specific regional craft revival, and
      eventually either leading a design team or running a small, stable label with a loyal repeat customer
      base. That version is genuinely achievable for a committed designer. Household-name fame is not
      something to plan a career around.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="alt-paths">Want the fashion world without the full design-degree grind?</h2>

  <p>
    Not everyone drawn to fashion actually needs a full design degree and years of sketching-and-sampling
    apprenticeship to build the career they actually want. Four adjacent paths use real fashion instincts
    and can start earning sooner or scale faster, depending on what genuinely interests you.
