    stronger income opportunities inside that room, or spend years waiting near the entrance.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ceiling">How to actually raise your income ceiling</h2>

  <p>
    A fair salary table only tells you where most people land, not where the strongest outcomes actually
    come from. Fashion design's income ceiling is not fixed by the degree; it is set by how far past
    "salaried design assistant" a person is willing and able to move.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      The clearest ceiling-raising moves inside this field are: moving into merchandising or buying
      leadership once design literacy and commercial numeracy both genuinely exist, developing a specialist
      niche like bridalwear, sustainable and technical textiles, or a specific regional craft revival, where
      genuinely few designers are competent, building a small, stable label once a real customer base and
      repeat orders exist rather than chasing scale too early, and pairing design skill with a digital-first
      personal brand that generates inbound client or brand-partnership interest instead of relying only on
      applications.
    </p>
    <p>
      None of these are guaranteed outcomes, and none happen from the degree alone. They happen for
      designers who pair craft depth with visible proof, commercial fluency, and enough business literacy
      to price and sell their own expertise, not just perform it for a fixed salary.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    For anyone genuinely leaning toward the own-label or D2C route, price the finished garment for what it
    solves for the customer, not just the hours of stitching behind it, and start with a small, fixed
    range of repeatable pieces rather than a sprawling custom-order menu that is hard to scale. Early
    clients and stockists usually come from referrals and a visible body of work, not cold outreach, so
    building a habit of asking happy customers for the next introduction matters more than it seems in the
    first year. It is also worth registering under Udyam early once orders become regular, since it is
    free, and checking current GST turnover thresholds before assuming registration can wait, both of
    which catch fashion founders off guard more often than fabric costs do.
  </p>

  <p>
    On AI specifically, the realistic path is staged, not a single leap. Right now, the useful move is
    learning to work alongside CAD and generative moodboarding tools so early concept development and
    trend research take a fraction of the time they used to. As adoption matures over the next few years,
    the bigger opportunity shifts toward owning the curation and judgment layer: verifying AI-generated
    concepts for real cultural and commercial fit, catching a fit or construction error a tool misses, and
    using freed-up time to take on more client-facing or specialist work instead of more routine sketching
    volume.
  </p>

  <h2 id="higher-studies">M.Des, MBA, or textile engineering: which backup is worth it</h2>

  <p>
    This question plays out differently for fashion design than for most branches, because the field
    rewards commercial and technical add-ons more than a second design-only credential.
  </p>

  <p>
    A postgraduate specialisation in fashion or textile design, buildable on top of a bachelor's degree,
    genuinely helps for a specific craft or technical direction, sustainable textiles, technical sportswear,
    or a design-education path. Separately, a fashion management or retail-focused MBA can matter, but it
    works best when layered on top of real industry experience, aimed specifically at merchandising, buying,
    or brand-leadership roles that value that domain depth.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      An M.Des genuinely helps for a specific specialisation or a move into teaching and design leadership,
      but it rarely changes outcomes much for a graduate who already has a strong portfolio and a clear
      industry-facing goal. An MBA is not a rescue plan for a weak core-degree profile; it works best when
      paired with real design or production experience, aimed at the commercial side of the same industry
      rather than treated as an escape from it.
    </p>
  </div>

